+# M3 plan · Measurement tools (2026-10-19)
+
+Goal (spec §5 M3): objective numbers for the later milestones, all testable without hardware. This plan settles
+every choice M3-02..M3-05 need: the WAV library API, the test signals, the wow-and-flutter algorithm, the telemetry
+log M7 will write (D11), drift, and how Mixxx's output is recorded. Build-only policy: the stubbed and synthetic
+tests are what a session must make pass; the one live step (M3-05 step 2) is written out under record-output.
+
+Machine facts were read on Michael's desktop on 2026-10-19 (read-only queries): `pw-record` 1.6.8 (libpipewire
+1.6.8), WirePlumber 0.5.12, `/usr/bin/ffmpeg`, Default Sink `alsa_output.usb-Rane_Rane_ONE_A-00.analog-surround-40`,
+**Default Source `alsa_input.usb-Rane_Rane_ONE_A-00.analog-surround-21`** (the Rane One's capture). The algorithm
+numbers below come from a scratch prototype run the same day (see Evidence). Nothing here is a MIDI fact; the
+telemetry fields that depend on the platter format (`ticks`, `timer`) inherit M4's UNVERIFIED status.
+
+All tools follow the M1 conventions: `usage: tools/<name> …` and exit 2 on bad arguments (add the cases to
+`tests/tools-usage.test.mjs`), exit 1 on a failed measurement or bad input, Node 24 with zero dependencies,
+tests write only under a temp dir, `*.wav` stays gitignored.
+
+## WAV library
+
+`tools/lib/wav.mjs`, pure ESM, no I/O except the two file wrappers. Samples are floats in [-1, 1], one typed
+array per channel (not interleaved), so analysis code never deals with byte layouts.
+
+```js
+export const FORMATS = ["pcm16", "pcm24", "pcm32", "float32"];
+// channels: array of Float32Array|Float64Array, equal lengths, values in [-1, 1].
+export function encodeWav({ sampleRate, format, channels }) // → Buffer
+export function decodeWav(buffer, { channel } = {})          // → { sampleRate, format, channels, frames, sizeFromEof }
+export function writeWav(path, opts)                         // encodeWav + writeFileSync (tmp + rename)
+export function readWav(path, opts)                          // readFileSync + decodeWav
+```
+
+- **Header written:** `RIFF`/`WAVE`, `fmt `, then `data`. Format tag 1 (PCM, 16-byte `fmt `) for `pcm16|24|32`;
+  tag 3 (IEEE float) for `float32` with an 18-byte `fmt ` (`cbSize` 0) and a `fact` chunk holding the frame
+  count, as the WAVE spec requires for non-PCM data. `byteRate = sampleRate · nChannels · bytesPerSample`, `blockAlign = nChannels ·
+  bytesPerSample`, little-endian, 24-bit packed in 3 bytes. Odd-sized data gets the RIFF pad byte.
+- **PCM scaling, both ways `2^(bits-1)`:** encode `round(x · 2^(bits-1))` clamped to `[-2^(bits-1),
+  2^(bits-1)-1]`; decode `v / 2^(bits-1)` into a `Float64Array` (exact for every format). A round trip is exact to
+  1 LSB, and full-scale negative decodes to exactly -1. Float32 is stored as is (NaN/±Inf rejected by `encodeWav`).
+- **Header read:** format tags 1, 3 and 0xFFFE (`WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE`; the sub-format GUID's first two bytes
+  give 1 or 3), PCM 16/24/32-bit, float 32-bit. Unknown chunks (`LIST`, `fact`, `PEAK`, which libsndfile and so
+  `pw-record` may write) are skipped, honouring the pad byte. `byteRate` and `blockAlign` must match the formula
+  above; a mismatch is an error, never guessed past.
+- **Streamed headers:** a `data` size of 0 or 0xFFFFFFFF, or one that runs past the end of the file (a
+  `pw-record` killed before it rewrote its header), is read up to EOF in whole frames, and `sizeFromEof: true`
+  says so. Callers print it as a warning.
+- **`{ channel }`:** decode only that channel (0-based), so a 5-minute stereo recording (14.4 M frames) costs one
+  `Float64Array` of about 115 MB, not two. Channels are always `Float64Array`, never `Float32Array`: float32 can't
+  hold a 32-bit PCM sample exactly (up to 128 LSB off above 2^24), which would break the pcm32 round trip.
+- Errors are thrown as `Error` with a message naming the file offset (`wav: fmt chunk at byte 12: …`); tools catch
+  them and exit 1.
+- Tests (`tests/wav.test.mjs`, M3-02 step 1): round trip of all four formats × mono/stereo at 48000 Hz on a ramp
+  that includes ±1; `byteRate`/`blockAlign` read back from the header bytes; an extensible header and an extra
+  `LIST` chunk built by hand in the test; a streamed header (size 0xFFFFFFFF); truncated and bad-tag files rejected.
+
+## make-test-audio
+
+`tools/make-test-audio <tone|clicks> [options]`, writing through `wav.mjs`. Defaults match M3-02: 48000 Hz,
+`pcm32`, stereo (both channels identical), 5 s.
+
+| Option | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `--seconds <s>` | 5 | Length; frames = `round(s · rate)` (5 s → 240000). |
+| `--out <file>` | `./<kind>.wav` | Output path; `-` writes the WAV to stdout. |
+| `--rate <hz>` | 48000 | Sample rate (the Rane One and the null sink are 48 kHz only). |
+| `--format <f>` | `pcm32` | One of `FORMATS`. |
+| `--channels <n>` | 2 | 1 or 2. |
+| `--level-db <dBFS>` | -6 | Peak level: amplitude `10^(dB/20)` (−6 → 0.5012). |
+| `--freq <hz>` | 1000 | `tone` only. |
+| `--fm-depth <percent>` `--fm-rate <hz>` | 0, 4 | `tone` only: frequency modulation, peak deviation in percent of `--freq`. |
+| `--bpm <n>` | 120 | `clicks` only. |
+
+- **tone:** `x(t) = A · sin(φ(t))` with the phase integrated, never `sin(2π f(t) t)` (that form's deviation grows
+  with t): `φ(t) = 2π f0 (t − D/(2π fm) · cos(2π fm t))`, `D = depth/100`. The instantaneous frequency is then
+  exactly `f0 (1 + D sin(2π fm t))`, so `--fm-depth 0.1 --fm-rate 4` is ±0.1 % at 4 Hz.
+- **clicks:** a 1 ms rectangular pulse (`round(rate/1000)` = 48 samples) at the peak level, starting at sample
+  `round(k · 60/bpm · rate)` for every beat that fits; silence elsewhere. 120 bpm × 5 s → 10 clicks (t = 0 … 4.5 s).
+  It is for latency and timing checks in M7/M8 and for testing click detection.
+- Prints one line to stderr: `wrote tone.wav: 240000 frames, 48000 Hz, pcm32, 2 ch`.
+- Tests (`tests/make-test-audio.test.mjs`, M3-02 step 3): frames 240000 for 5 s; peak within 1 % of 0.5012; 10
+  clicks counted as rising edges at 120 bpm, with an implicit `x[-1] = 0` (the first click starts at sample 0,
+  so a count that ignores it finds 9); the FM tone's rising zero-crossing count within 0.2 % of the plain
+  tone's.
+
+## wow-flutter algorithm
+
+`tools/wow-flutter <file.wav> [--channel 0] [--json] [--expect-hz <f>]`. Method: **zero-crossing period
+estimation with linear interpolation, per-cycle frequency, a 0.5 Hz high-pass to remove the pitch offset, then
+peak and RMS deviation in percent.** Steps:
+
+1. **Read** one channel (`--channel`, default 0) with `readWav`. If its RMS is below -60 dBFS, or it has fewer
+   than 3 s of good cycles (step 5; 1 s is skipped at each end and one full 1 s window must remain), print `no signal` (`{"signal": false}` with `--json`) and **exit 1**. Silence from the
+   null sink must give this, never a crash or a division by zero (M3-05 step 2).
+2. **DC:** subtract the channel mean.
+3. **Zero crossings:** every rising crossing `x[i] < 0 ≤ x[i+1]` at time `t = (i + x[i]/(x[i] − x[i+1])) / rate`
+   (linear interpolation between the two samples). One crossing per cycle.
+4. **Per-cycle frequency:** `f_k = 1 / (t_{k+1} − t_k)`, and the cycle's peak `|x|` between its crossings.
+5. **Dropouts:** a cycle is bad when its period differs from the median period by more than 5 % or its peak is
+   below half the median cycle peak. Each run of consecutive bad cycles counts as one **dropout**. Bad cycles
+   and the 2 cycles on each side of them are excluded from every statistic. For the filter, their deviation is
+   bridged by linear interpolation between the neighbouring good cycles. A 1 ms run of zeros therefore shows up
+   as `dropouts: 1`, not as flutter (M3-03 step 3).
+6. **Mean frequency** `f̄ = (number of good cycles) / (sum of their periods)`. This is the reported "mean Hz" (1010
+   Hz tone → 1010.000). `--expect-hz f` fails (exit 1) if `f̄` is more than 5 % off `f`, which catches the wrong
+   file or a 45/33 mix-up.
+7. **Deviation series** `d_k = f_k / f̄ − 1`, treated as uniformly sampled at `f̄` (cycles are within 1 % of
+   uniform, so the error is far below the tolerances).
+8. **0.5 Hz high-pass (removes the pitch offset and slow drift):** the passband starts at 0.5 Hz. It is a
+   2nd-order Butterworth biquad (RBJ cookbook, Q = 1/√2) with its −3 dB corner at **0.2 Hz**, run forward then
+   backward (zero phase, net gain = |H|²). Both ends are padded with 3 s of odd reflection (`2·d_0 − d_k`, as
+   scipy's `filtfilt` does). Net gain: 0.975 at 0.5 Hz, 1.000 at 4 Hz, 0.059 at 0.1 Hz, 0 at DC.
+   *Why not a −3 dB corner at 0.5 Hz:* run zero-phase, it halves a 0.5 Hz wow. The prototype measured 0.252 %
+   for a 0.5 % @ 0.5 Hz tone, which fails M3-03's 0.42–0.58 % window. "0.5 Hz high-pass" in this project means
+   the band edge (≥ 97 % passed). D12 records it.
+9. **Statistics** over the filtered series, skipping the first and last 1 s (filter edges) and excluded cycles:
+   **peak %** = `100 · max |d|`, **RMS %** = `100 · sqrt(mean d²)`. Unweighted. The upper band edge is half the
+   cycle rate (500 Hz for 1 kHz), and per-cycle averaging costs −0.0002 dB at 4 Hz.
+10. **Worst 1-second window:** windows of 1 s at a 0.1 s hop. The window with the largest RMS is reported with
+    its start time, peak and RMS.
+
+Output (text): `mean 1000.000 Hz  peak 0.1008 %  rms 0.0707 %  worst 1 s @ 2.30 s: peak 0.1008 % rms 0.0707 %
+dropouts 0  cycles 4999`. `--json`: `{"signal":true,"mean_hz":…,"peak_pct":…,"rms_pct":…,"worst":{"start_s":…,
+"peak_pct":…,"rms_pct":…},"dropouts":…,"cycles":…,"size_from_eof":false}`.
+
+**Tolerances the tests must meet** (M3-03 step 1; feature step: synthetic 0.1 % FM at 4 Hz measured within ±15 %
+of 0.1 %, i.e. peak 0.085–0.115 %). Prototype results with the algorithm above on 48 kHz tones:
+
+| Synthetic tone | Must report | Prototype |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 kHz clean, 5 s (float and 16-bit) | peak and RMS < 0.01 % | 0.0000 % / 0.0000 % |
+| 1 kHz, 0.1 % FM at 4 Hz, 5 s | peak 0.085–0.115 % | peak 0.1008 %, RMS 0.0707 % |
+| 1 kHz, 0.5 % FM at 0.5 Hz, **10 s** | peak 0.42–0.58 % | peak 0.4913 % |
+| 1010 Hz clean, 5 s (pitch offset) | deviation < 0.01 %, mean 1010 ± 0.5 Hz | peak 0.0013 %, mean 1010.000 Hz |
+| 1 kHz clean + 1 ms of zeros at 2.5 s | `dropouts: 1`, deviation < 0.01 % | dropouts 1, peak 0.0000 % |
+
+Notes for M3-03: give the 0.5 Hz FM test ≥ 10 s (a whole number of FM cycles). At 5 s the mean is biased (the
+prototype got 1000.637 Hz), though the peak still passed. The 0.0013 % on the 1010 Hz tone is linear
+interpolation error (crossings drift across the sample grid). It is 8× under the tolerance, so don't "fix" it with
+a fancier interpolator. Weighting (IEC 60386's 4 Hz-centred curve), which spec §5 calls "if feasible", is
+deferred: a `--weighted` flag could be added later without changing these numbers.
+
+## Telemetry format
+
+M7 adds a debug setting to the mapping that writes platter telemetry; `tools/drift` (M3-04) reads it. The format
+is fixed now (D11 *default*) so the tool and the mapping can be built independently.
+
+- **File:** `dev-profile/telemetry.jsonl` (`~/.var/app/org.mixxx.Mixxx/dev-profile/telemetry.jsonl`), one JSON
+  object per line, UTF-8, `\n`-terminated, in time order.
+- **Keys (all required):**
+
+| Key | Type | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `t_us` | integer | Host time in µs when the platter message was handled (M7: `Date.now() * 1000`, so ms resolution in practice; no finer clock is known in QJSEngine, M7 checks). It is wall-clock time, so it can step back (NTP). |
+| `ticks` | integer | Unwrapped encoder position from the platter core (M4 decoder), in encoder ticks since the deck's telemetry started. Signed: backward motion lowers it. |
+| `timer` | integer | Unwrapped controller timestamp counter (M4 decoder; hypothesis 2,822,400 Hz, UNVERIFIED until M5). |
+| `playposition` | number | Mixxx's `[ChannelN],playposition` at that moment, nominally 0..1 (Mixxx can report slightly outside it before the track start; accepted). |
+| `rate_ratio` | number | The platter speed ratio the core's estimator reported (1.0 = nominal RPM). |
+| `mode` | string | `clock` (Mixxx plays at the pitch fader's rate) or `platter` (the platter drives `scratch_position`). |
+
+- **Optional:** `deck` (1 or 2; absent means 1) and `duration` (the track length in s, Mixxx's
+  `[ChannelN],duration` at full precision). Readers ignore unknown keys, so M7 may add more without breaking
+  `tools/drift`. A missing required key or a wrong type is an error naming the line number
+  (`telemetry.jsonl:12: missing "ticks"`). A `t_us` lower than the line before is a warning, not an error:
+  the reader clamps it to the previous value and counts it (`t_backsteps`), so one clock step doesn't throw away
+  a session.
+- **Rate:** M7 writes one line per deck at most every 50 ms (20 Hz, configurable), so 5 minutes is about 6,000
+  lines per deck. `playposition` only changes once per audio buffer, so every sample, the first one included,
+  lags by 0 to 1 buffer. The seed's `soundconfig.xml` has `latency="5"`: the buffer's length in ms is
+  UNVERIFIED (perhaps about 21 ms; M7 reads it from the log). `tools/drift` smooths for this (below).
+- **M7 must keep the sampling jitter small; smoothing can't fix it.** The scratch prototype (Evidence) added a
+  random 0–21 ms lag to every `playposition` of a zero-drift 5-minute log at 20 Hz. After the 1 s running median,
+  max |drift| was still 7.7–10.2 ms (3 seeds); with 2 s, 5.0–7.2 ms; with 5 s, 3.3–4.4 ms. That is the whole
+  10 ms budget of spec §3. So M7 writes a line from an `engine.makeConnection(group, "playposition", …)`
+  callback, i.e. when the engine publishes a new position. It pairs that with the platter position the core
+  extrapolates to the same `t_us`, rather than reading `playposition` at an arbitrary MIDI message. How tight
+  that callback's timing is stays UNVERIFIED until M7 measures it with `noise_ms` (below).
+- **How it gets to the file (M7):** controller scripts can't write files: `res/controllers/engine-api.d.ts` and `console-api.d.ts`
+  on the `2.5` branch (read 2026-10-19) offer settings, controls, connections, timers, scratch and logging, and no
+  file API. So the mapping logs each object as
+  `console.log("RaneOne telemetry " + JSON.stringify(obj))`. Under `--controller-debug` that reaches
+  `mixxx.log` as `HH:MM:SS.mmm Debug [Controller] RaneOne telemetry {…}` (the same path as `RaneOne: init`, seen
+  in M1-05). A host-side step extracts lines matching `^\S+ Debug \[Controller\] RaneOne telemetry (\{.*\})$`
+  into `dev-profile/telemetry.jsonl`. Proposed owner: a `tools/mixxx-dev telemetry` subcommand in M7. Every
+  line is flushed (`--log-flush-level debug`, D9), so a hard stop loses nothing.
+
+## drift
+
+`tools/drift <telemetry.jsonl> --track-seconds <n> [--ticks-per-rev 3600] [--rpm 33.333] [--deck 1]
+[--jump-ms 20] [--smooth-ms 1000] [--csv] [--json]`. Spec §3: drift with the motor on, 5 min, target < 10 ms.
+
+- **`--rpm`:** `33`, `33.3`, `33.33`, `33.333` and `100/3` all mean exactly 100/3; `45` means 45; any other
+  positive number is taken as given. A literal 33.333 is 1.0e-5 low, which alone would be 3 ms of fake drift over
+  5 min, 30 % of the budget.
+- **`--track-seconds`:** must be the exact duration (frames / rate). A value rounded to 0.5 s on a 6-minute
+  track fakes about 0.4 s of drift over 5 min. When the lines carry `duration` (D11), a command-line value that
+  differs from it by more than 1 ms is a warning, and the command-line value is used.
+- **Definition:** for each line `i` of the chosen deck, relative to the first line:
+  - `platter_s = (ticks_i − ticks_0) / ticks_per_rev · 60 / rpm`: track seconds the platter has turned through.
+    One revolution at the nominal RPM is 60/rpm seconds of record, whatever the motor's actual speed.
+  - `mixxx_s = (playposition_i − playposition_0) · track_seconds`
+  - `raw_ms = 1000 · (mixxx_s − platter_s)`. Positive means Mixxx is ahead of the platter.
+  - **Smoothing against the per-buffer lag of `playposition`:** `drift_ms` = the centred running median of
+    `raw_ms` over `--smooth-ms` (default 1000; 0 turns it off), minus the median of `raw_ms` over the first
+    `--smooth-ms`. One lagged first sample therefore can't offset the whole series.
+- **Reported:** `max` = max |drift_ms|, `final` = the last drift_ms, `slope` = least-squares slope of `raw_ms`
+  against t in **ms/min**. `jumps`: at every sample, the median of `raw_ms` over the `--smooth-ms` after it minus
+  the median over the `--smooth-ms` before it. Each run of samples where that exceeds `--jump-ms` (default 20 ms)
+  is one jump, reported at the sample with the largest change, with its time in s from the start and its size.
+  Comparing medians keeps buffer jitter from looking like jumps: with 0–21 ms random lag, a 50 ms step at 120 s
+  came back as exactly one jump within 0.3 s in 5 of 5 seeds. **`noise_ms`** = 1.4826 · median |raw_ms −
+  drift_ms|, the robust spread of the samples around the smoothed curve. It's printed next to `max` so a reader
+  can see when `max` is mostly sampling noise (see Telemetry format). Also: sample count, span in s, mean
+  `rate_ratio`, number of `mode` changes, `t_backsteps`.
+- **`--csv`:** prints `t_us,drift_ms` (header, then one row per sample, smoothed) to stdout instead of the
+  summary.
+  `--json`: the summary as one object.
+- Assumption to confirm in M5/M8: the drift is meaningful only if the One's motor speed follows the pitch fader,
+  since Mixxx plays at the fader's rate. If the motor ignores the fader, drift grows by design whenever the fader
+  isn't at 0, and M7 has to handle that.
+- Not handled by the tool: a 33/45 switch inside one log (use one `--rpm` per log) and track loops or cue jumps,
+  which show up as jumps, correctly.
+- Tests (M3-04 step 1), synthetic logs written by the test at 20 Hz: zero drift → max < 0.1 ms; 10 ms accumulated
+  linearly over 300 s → final 10 ± 0.5 ms and slope 2 ± 0.1 ms/min; a 50 ms step at t = 120 s → one jump
+  reported within 1 s of 120 s. Also: the jump log with a seeded random 0–21 ms lag added to `playposition`
+  still reports exactly one jump within 1 s, and the zero-drift log with that lag reports `noise_ms` > 1; `--rpm 33.333` on a log made with 100/3
+  gives max < 0.1 ms; a bad line → exit 1 naming its line number; a backward `t_us` → a warning and
+  `t_backsteps: 1`.
+
+## record-output
+
+`tools/record-output [--seconds 10] [--target mixxx-dev.monitor] <out.wav>` records the null sink's monitor, so
+Mixxx's output is measured without speakers or the Rane One. Recording is capture only: `pw-record` creates an
+input stream and plays nothing.
+
+- **The exact command:**
+  `pw-record --target mixxx-dev.monitor --rate 48000 --format s32 --channels 2 <file.wav>`.
+  The tool runs that command with two additions:
+  - `--sample-count <seconds · 48000>`: pw-record 1.6.8's `-n`, "Stop after COUNT samples". M3-05's live 2 s
+    check confirms that COUNT means frames (expect 96000 frames).
+  - `-P node.dont-fallback=true`: if the target doesn't resolve, WirePlumber doesn't link the stream to the
+    default source, which is the **Rane One's capture**. It sends "defined target not found" and **destroys the
+    node** (`find-defined-target.lua` lines 116–123, unless `node.linger` is set), so `pw-record` most likely
+    exits early with a header-only WAV.
+  So whenever `pw-record` exits, the tool reads the WAV back and requires exactly `seconds · 48000` frames at
+  48000 Hz, 2 ch. Otherwise it exits 1 with "pw-record recorded N of M frames: target not found or not linked?"
+  and deletes the short file, so an empty file can never pass for measured silence. A timeout of `seconds + 5` s
+  kills `pw-record` and fails the same way.
+- **Refusals (exit 3):** a `--target` containing `Rane` (case-insensitive), `alsa_input`, `alsa_output`, or one
+  that doesn't end in `.monitor`. A missing `mixxx-dev` sink (`tools/null-sink status` exits 1) also exits 1
+  with "run tools/null-sink up". The tool never loads or unloads a sink itself and never touches defaults.
+- **Expected problem with the target name, for M3-05 to settle live:** WirePlumber 0.5.12 resolves
+  `target.object` against `node.name` or `object.path` only
+  (`/usr/share/wireplumber/scripts/linking/find-defined-target.lua`, lines 82–91). The null sink's node is named
+  `mixxx-dev`, and `mixxx-dev.monitor` is a name only `pipewire-pulse` gives to Pulse clients. So
+  `--target mixxx-dev.monitor` is expected **not** to resolve for the native `pw-record`. Its monitor is reached
